 133.333%;"><img alt=3D"I almost gave up during this trip. =E2=AC=87=EF=B8=
=8F

I just hiked from the Mediterranean Sea to the Sea of Galilee, 50mi (80km) =
in three days. I=E2=80=99ve been wanting to hike this trail for months now =
and I finally had the chance to. It was a test of strength and also a reall=
y valuable push out of my comfort zone.=20

The first night of the trip I was alone, camping right next to the road nea=
r a random town. I=E2=80=99d set up camp in the dark, there were blinding l=
ights from passing cars, and that was the moment where I started to realize=
 the magnitude of what I was doing. I was sore, unconfident in my ability, =
and honestly a little scared. I went to bed unsure if I was going to contin=
ue the next day or if it was better to take the bail out, before the busses=
 and trains stopped for Shabbat.=20

But I knew that I was prepared, and that this was the only time I had to do=
 this trail before I return to the US. So I woke up the next day and contin=
ued.=20

And thank goodness I did. The next day I met new people, and as we talked, =
shared stories, shared coffee, we also shared the weight that we were carry=
ing. I started alone, but I ended it with people who I will remember foreve=
r. The memories I made in the last few days will last a lifetime and the pu=
sh that I gave myself makes me more excited to do a trip like this again, e=
ven as I lay in bed more sore than I=E2=80=99ve ever been.=20

So let this be a lesson: if you want to do something, go do it. Don=E2=80=
=99t wait for someone else to give you permission, do it alone if you have =
no one to do it with. It will all be worth it in the end =F0=9F=92=9B=F0=9F=
